# Markdown: block quotes styled with For element: Block quote and horizontal rules styled with For element: Divider.
Document (dev.stxt.pdf):
	Language: en
	Title: Markdown · Block quotes and dividers
	Page:
		Paper: A4
		Margin: 20mm
	Styles:
		For element: Block quote
			Box:
				Margin: 4 0 12 0
				Padding: 2 0 2 12
				Border: 0 0 0 3
				Border color: accent
			Typography:
				Font: IBM Plex Serif
				Italic: true
		For element: Divider
			Box:
				Margin: 12 60
				Border: 1 0 0 0
				Border color: rule
	Section:
		Text: Markdown
			Style: topic
		Heading 1:
			Text: Block quotes and dividers
		Paragraph: A `>` quote is a `Block quote` and a `---` line is a `Divider`. Both are generated, so only their `For element` rules style them. Without a rule a quote looks like a paragraph and a divider is a line of 0.5 across the width.
			Style: intro
		Markdown:
			Bullet markers: bullet
			Content >>
				The visitors' book of the workshop keeps this note:

				> The chairs have outlived the table they were made for.
				> We bought a new table to match them.

				---

				A quote can hold several paragraphs and a list:

				> First paragraph of the quote.
				>
				> - an item
				> - another item

				***

				The three forms of the rule, `---`, `***` and `___`, are the same element.
